<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">BEGONE,&nbsp;THOUGHT!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The list really bugs me, because it references a lot of things that aren't explained, or are otherwise nonsensical. For instance:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A modified Mark 4 nuclear bomb. Light, sound, and radioactivity produced by SCP-4000-D99 do not exit an 80m radius, instead being reflected back towards the center of the explosion.</p>
<p>Stored as a 43.1TB executable file inside SCP-4000-M32, which is located inside SCP-4000-β.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We don't learn what M32 is, or why it can store a nuclear bomb as data.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Forces SCP-4000-G35 into a state of constant prayer, immobilizing it so that cerebrospinal fluid extraction may continue.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Same here. We don't learn what G35 is, or why its cerebrospinal fluid is necessary.</p>
<p>At the same time&#8230; all of these nonsensical components kind of remind me of the concept of the God Machine from World of Darkness, combined with Warehouse 13. That makes me want to upvote it&#8230;</p>
<p>But this line alone makes me conflicted enough to no-vote, leaning towards downvote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Throughout the Trail of Tears, a regrettable yet sadly necessary event, many objects left in the haste of the natives were taken and utilized.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;The Trail of Tears&quot; and &quot;necessary event&quot; do not mesh in my mind.</p>
<p>After this, the skip gets rather&#8230; shall we say, reductionist. I strongly dislike &quot;This one thing caused all of these other things&quot; trope that's presented after Norton disagrees with the atom bomb. History is more complex than that. Plus, the whole &quot;America is at the center of history&quot; is an idea that needs to die&#8212; if this idea is meant to be deconstructed/satirical (which may be the case, knowing the two authors), then I fail to see the satire or deconstruction in it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>This is all a long way of saying &quot;I'm no-voting, leaning towards downvote&quot;.</p>

							</div>

								
