<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>We don't learn what M32 is, or why it can store a nuclear bomb as data. [&#8230;] Same here. We don't learn what G35 is, or why its cerebrospinal fluid is necessary.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm pretty sure that's the point. It's demonstrating the immense scale and complexity of the machine by oblique references to unseen mechanisms, forcing the reader to speculate why these components would be necessary the same way the Foundation is when they're trying to contain this thing without a comprehensive understanding of how or why it works.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After this, the skip gets rather… shall we say, reductionist. I strongly dislike &quot;This one thing caused all of these other things&quot; trope that's presented after Norton disagrees with the atom bomb.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this is also intentional and criticized throughout the article. The people containing SCP-4000 are those who believe wholeheartedly in the imperialist mission of the United States. They have no compunction about what they've done, the wars they've waged, the cruelty they've enabled, the cultural artifacts they've stolen to assemble their great device.</p>
<p>Of course, to them, when things start going wrong, do they take responsibility and acknowledge the true complexity of their actions, that they aren't really leading a black and white &quot;crusade&quot; against evil? No, they blame Norton. They are unable to confront the fact that they might be responsible and choose instead to hide behind a scapegoat.</p>
<p>Is the &quot;rise of terror&quot; a result of America's constant destabilization of the Middle East for their own benefit, or is it because Emperor Norton did an oopsie? When a diplomatic standoff nearly destroys the world, should America reconsider their nuclear policies or was it just Norton's fault?</p>
<p>When people start to wise up and criticize the global imperialism of the US, is it because the US has done something wrong? It can't be, because they've never done anything wrong. It's just that their propaganda machine is malfunctioning.</p>
<p>The Foundation doesn't acknowledge the complexity because they're not capable of it.</p>
</div>
