<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I despise any depiction of the Foundation as a monolithic, incompetent, shallow entity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't at all what I'm saying. I'm not saying they're too stupid to realize it, I'm saying their organization is inherently unable to deal with this issue due to their history and structure. It's an institutional issue. This much is clearly defined in the article, which also establishes this interpretation of the Foundation as incredibly closely linked to the United States government.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Stories like this seem to forget that the Foundation is made up of people from hundreds of countries and thousands of backgrounds, and that they're, you know, human beings and not every human being is a deluded psychopath, and not every member of the Foundation is an American who would go along with this.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a really confusing critique to have considering that there are literally countless examples of this happening all throughout history and every single day today. Institutions regularly commit horrible atrocities completely independent of whether or not each individual member of the institution is a psychopathic monster. Most of them probably just don't critically consider their actions, or believe they're doing the right thing regardless. That is how institutional issues tend to work.</p>
<p>American imperialism is a thing that happened and continues to happen. &quot;But not everyone involved is a monster&quot; has never changed or stopped it at any point, ever. America being a diverse nation has never stopped racist policies at home, nor imperialist wars abroad. If you find that hard to believe, all I can say is that you should do more research.</p>
